ne  similar to Urdu perhaps Chad or herself  or the Hime reminded once again of what  he needs to protect Ichigo throws  caution to the wind and makes contact  with ginjo and this is where he learns  how to use his full bring power and it's  where that clip of him with that  questionably large emblem comes from  also this section of the arc is  essentially about Ichigo trying to  regain the agency he's since lost both  literally and narratively the training  itself takes him to interesting  situations from being shrunk down into a  dollhouse fighting a yakuza's soul in  the body of a stuffed animal all the way  to fighting ginjou himself inside a  video game okay wow you're throwing a  lot at me here with Ichigo coming to  learn that the very Deputy badge he's  been carrying around is the key to his  full-bring ability and by extension the  restoration of his abilities symbolizing  a time where he had friendships he's  missed and Powers he could use to  protect them with however curiously once  again following his training he hears  arukia's voice following it forcing us  to contend with the possibility that  that dream where he thought he could  hear her was in fact linked to his badge  in some way  the best part  okay okay it was time to talk about the  best part of this Arc and by far the  part that gave me the most joy as I  mentioned there are a lot of ideas that  compound to make this Arc feel special  however around 60 to 75 of the way  through the arc itself following the  training reprieve the story itself does  something amazing by shifting and  transforming from what we've come to  know and expect from bleach into this  horrific psychological Thriller type  narrative seriously it's unlike anything  I've seen from this series and maybe  also unlike anything I've seen or come  across in both one piece Naruto Dragon  Ball or whatever it's such a unique idea  and it's explored in such a captivating  fashion I've always said that bleach  first and foremost has felt like a very  character-driven story with it producing  less interesting material when it takes  the focus off of the characters  themselves and so with full brings  introduction of this tonal shift it  takes what otherwise felt like a  refreshing Slice of Life reprieved from  the world ending Stakes of the prior Arc  into a set of circumstances that  genuinely felt more high stakes than  when ice reason was front and center for  me and that's not because I don't know  the difference in scale between the  world at stake and ichigo's life being  at stake but because each go is our  means of seeing through into this world  it's like our very existence in this  plane that we've now invested almost 500  chapters into is being stolen from us as  readers right before our eyes like [ __ ]  you tsukushima you didn't save Rukia you  didn't save Orihime or defeat Eisen we I  mean Ichigo did and while I think ginjo  is the MVP of this Arc I think  tsukishima is the other half of that  coin which grants this arcade level of  dread and excitement that for me has  been unmatched in this series one by one  we see him pick off ichigo's comrades  with his brush with Orihime being  particularly curious as this is where we  first start to get a taste of what his  power truly is with her not only  appearing perfectly fine physically  after what appeared to be a stabbing  through her heart but that she also  suddenly thought of him tsukushima as a  friend and as a Time takes on by and  conflicts fly past slowly but surely one  after another ichigo's former allies  suddenly appear to change not physically  but psychologically with the very fact  that these words are coming from his  friends making it all the more  unsettling showing up in his home his  place of work and on the street  tsukushima is less of an imposing force  of Nature and more so a ghostly specter  or spirit that follows Ichigo until he's  exhausted every support system he  possesses creating a scenario of not  just